"""        <h2>The Scene <span class="em">🎬</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">The question stings precisely because you've done everything right — and it mistakes the accent for the expertise.</p>

        <p>Let's be honest about what's happening, because the lesson only works honestly. Sometimes "someone English" is prejudice in a blazer. But often — more often — it's something more ordinary: a stressed customer who half-missed two sentences, on a bad phone line, with money on the line, reaching for the version of confidence he grew up with. He doesn't actually need a native speaker. He needs to feel understood and safe — and those are things you can deliver in the next ninety seconds, because they were never about the accent.</p>

        <p>The three traps, in order of temptation. Fold — handing the call to a native colleague who knows nothing about this client's system, which punishes the customer with worse service and teaches your company your expertise is optional. Fight — turning it into a confrontation about prejudice, which may be deserved but loses the account and, worse, your standing as the calm one. Or crumble — apologising for your English in a voice that suddenly does wobble, confirming exactly the doubt he voiced.</p>

        <p>The fourth way: treat it as a communication complaint, not an identity verdict. Take the useful grain — maybe you were fast, maybe the jargon was thick — fix THAT visibly, and re-anchor the conversation to the thing he actually needs: the person who knows his file. Which is you. It was always you.</p>

        <ol class="bigq">
            <li>Where's the line between a customer struggling to understand and a customer who's decided not to? How can you tell from inside the call?</li>
            <li>"They asked for a native speaker" — who loses most if that request is granted: you, the client, or the colleague who inherits a file they don't know?</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="whos">
            <div class="who"><b>You</b><span>Senior engineer, eleven years' experience, this client's system in your head — and an accent from Porto that has never once misdiagnosed a fault.</span></div>
            <div class="who"><b>Mr Whitmore</b><span>The client. Production line down, money burning, patience gone. Not a monster — a frightened man reaching for a familiar-sounding lifeline.</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="tip"><b>First:</b> read the conversation and watch what You's voice does after the sting: it slows down and gets MORE authoritative, not less. Then find the sentence that turns the whole call — it's about the file, not the accent.</div>""",

